    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d353">

                <pb facs="0342" n="328" sameAs="frus:frus1942v06/pg328"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.61351/3150: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Braden</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-01T18:00:00-04:00">July 1, 1942—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 2—12:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>489. For Walmsley. Department’s instruction no. 169, June 25. The Institute has
                    accepted without change Defense Supplies Corporation letter of June 25 relative
                    to the 10,000,000 gallons of blackstrap. Moreover, at the Embassy’s suggestion
                    both the Prime Minister and Hevia<anchor n="12" xml:id="d353-f1"/> have signed
                    the acceptance. The letter will be returned by air mail tomorrow.</p>
                <p>All the officials concerned have expressed their sincere appreciation of our
                    Government’s cooperation in this matter. I hope appropriate instructions to
                    release the blackstrap will immediately be issued.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Braden</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d353-f1" n="12" xml:id="d353-f1-content">
                <p>Carlos Hevia, Chairman of the Cuban Price Control Commission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
